{"id": "q2", "claim": "c1", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s1", "s3"], "question": "The introduction claims that a node's functional role provides evidence about whether it is important. In S³martCirc's training, the Important Node Classification stage (Eq. 4) never touches one of the three masking coefficients. Which learnable coefficients does that stage update, and for which nodes? How can such an update change the set of nodes that the next Important Node Discovery step treats as part of the circuit?", "answer": "The classification loss sums only over the current important-node set N_i and updates only the passthrough and functional coefficients (c_p and c_f), not c_u. Discovery treats a node as a candidate, and leaves it unmasked, when c_p + c_f > c_u. Raising or lowering c_p and c_f therefore shifts that sum relative to c_u, which can move a node into or out of the candidate set for the next discovery step. This is the route by which role information feeds back into importance.", "rubric": ["States that the classification stage acts only on nodes in the current important/candidate set and updates only their passthrough and functional coefficients (c_p, c_f), not the unimportant coefficient c_u", "States that candidacy/masking in discovery depends on comparing c_p + c_f with c_u", "Concludes that classification updates can therefore flip a node's candidate status for the next discovery step"]},
{"id": "q3", "claim": "c1", "kind": "claim", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "textbase", "sections": ["s1", "s3"], "question": "Which component of S³martCirc is the paper's answer to the second of the two challenges named in its introduction? Apply that component, as the paper defines it, to a node processing a two-token input. The cosine similarity between the two input token vectors is 0.8, and the cosine similarity between the node's two output token vectors is 0.6. What value does the component produce, and why does producing such a value address that challenge?", "answer": "The second challenge is subjective functional roles, and the component that answers it is the FI metric (Eq. 3), the normalized change in the token-wise similarity matrix. With two tokens, Sim(X) has off-diagonal entries of 0.8 and Sim(Y) has off-diagonal entries of 0.6. FI is therefore ||Sim(X)−Sim(Y)||_F / ||Sim(X)||_F = (√2·0.2)/(√2·0.8) = 0.25. Because FI is a number computed directly from activations, it can enter the classification loss, which replaces subjective human judgment of roles with a quantitative, optimizable signal.", "rubric": ["Identifies the FI (functional interpretation) metric as the answer to the subjective-roles challenge", "Computes FI = 0.25, i.e. the change in similarity (0.2) divided by the input similarity (0.8)", "Explains that a numeric, activation-derived value can be placed in the optimization objective instead of relying on subjective human judgment"]},
{"id": "q5", "claim": "c2", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s2"], "question": "A critic argues as follows: 'In the paper's mapping of the IOI circuit onto its two general roles, the S-inhibition heads and the negative name mover heads both deal with name/subject information, so they should receive the same general role.' Which role does the paper actually give each of these head types, and why does the paper's basis for its dichotomy reject the critic's reasoning?", "answer": "The paper labels the S-inhibition heads as passthrough nodes, because they route subject-related information to downstream components. It labels the negative name mover heads as functional nodes, because they transform information to selectively shape name logits. The dichotomy is defined by how a node processes information (relocating it versus transforming it), not by what content it carries. Sharing name-related content is therefore irrelevant to the label.", "rubric": ["States that S-inhibition heads are labeled passthrough", "States that negative name mover heads are labeled functional", "Explains that the dichotomy is based on how a node processes information (routing versus transforming), not on its content, so shared name content does not imply the same role"]},
{"id": "q10", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3"], "question": "Consider the paper's Important Node Classification objective (Eq. 4) for a single candidate node whose FI value is 0.25. Compare the rate at which the loss decreases per unit increase in the node's passthrough coefficient with the rate per unit increase in its functional coefficient. By what factor do they differ, and which role is favored? At what FI value would the two rates be equal?", "answer": "In Eq. 4 the c_f term is −c_f·FI and the c_p term is −c_p/FI. Per unit increase, c_f reduces the loss by FI = 0.25 and c_p reduces it by 1/FI = 4. The passthrough rate is therefore 16 times larger, and the node is pushed toward passthrough. The two rates are equal when FI = 1/FI, that is, at FI = 1.", "rubric": ["States that the reward per unit is FI (0.25) for the functional coefficient and 1/FI (4) for the passthrough coefficient, so the factor is 16", "Concludes that the node is pushed toward the passthrough role", "Identifies FI = 1 as the point where neither role is favored"]},
{"id": "q11", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s2", "s3"], "question": "Take a feedforward-neuron node in the paper's computational graph, with the output dimension the paper specifies for such nodes, processing a two-token input. Its two input token vectors have cosine similarity 0.5, and its outputs for the two tokens have opposite signs. Using the paper's similarity operator, what are the off-diagonal entries of Sim(Y), and what FI does the node receive? Why is it legitimate for FI to compare Sim(Y) with Sim(X) for this node at all?", "answer": "The paper sets d_n = 1 for feedforward neurons, so row-wise L2 normalization reduces each token's output to ±1. With opposite signs, the off-diagonal entries of Sim(Y) are −1. FI = |0.5 − (−1)| / 0.5 = 3. The comparison is legitimate because Sim(X) and Sim(Y) are both T×T token-by-token matrices, so they can be subtracted even though the input dimension d_m differs from the output dimension of 1.", "rubric": ["Notes that a feedforward neuron's output dimension is 1, so normalized outputs are ±1 and the off-diagonal entries of Sim(Y) are −1 here", "Computes FI = 1.5/0.5 = 3", "Explains that both similarity matrices are T×T (token by token), so they are comparable regardless of feature dimension"]},
{"id": "q27", "claim": "c4", "kind": "integration", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s6"], "question": "A node is evaluated on two tasks. On both tasks, ||Sim(X) − Sim(Y)||_F = 3. On task A, ||Sim(X)||_F = 2, and on task B, ||Sim(X)||_F = 8. Using the paper's FI definition and its classification objective, toward which role is the node pushed on each task? Which limitation stated in the paper's conclusion does this illustrate?", "answer": "FI divides the similarity change by ||Sim(X)||_F, so FI is 3/2 = 1.5 on task A and 3/8 = 0.375 on task B. Under Eq. 4 the functional term is weighted by FI and the passthrough term by 1/FI, so the node is pushed toward functional on task A (FI > 1) and toward passthrough on task B (FI < 1). This illustrates the conclusion's limitation: roles are defined relative to a task's activations rather than intrinsically, so the same node can be labeled differently across tasks.", "rubric": ["Computes FI as the change divided by the input-similarity norm: 1.5 on task A and 0.375 on task B", "Concludes functional on task A and passthrough on task B, because Eq. 4 favors functional when FI > 1 and passthrough when FI < 1", "Links this to the stated limitation that roles are task-relative rather than intrinsic"]}
]}
